<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-1-1-purpose">
  <time minutes="45" />
  <title>Purpose and Behavior</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <remark>
      <title>1.1.A</title>
      <p>
        Determine the purpose and behavior of an app by interacting with it.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>1.1.B</title>
      <p>
        Identify inputs and outputs of an app while using it.
      </p>
    </remark>

  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Purpose and Behavior of Apps</title>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.1</title>
      <p>Applications (apps) are programs that run on many kinds of computing devices.
        Apps can run on smartphones, tablets, laptops, desktops, game consoles, and web browsers,
        and they are designed to help users accomplish tasks or goals.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.2</title>
      <p>
        People use apps to help solve real problems and accomplish everyday tasks,
        such as communicating, learning, navigating, creating and sharing media, staying organized,
        and being entertained.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.3</title>
      <p>An app is successful when it uses information to produce an output that helps a user
        achieve
        a goal. An app’s value to the user depends on how well the results meet their needs.
      </p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.4</title>
      <p>Because people rely on apps for everyday tasks, app behavior matters.
        When an app is confusing, unreliable, or does not work as expected, users can become
        frustrated because it interrupts what they are trying to do.
      </p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.5</title>

      <p>An app’s behavior is what it does while it is running, including how it responds to user
        interactions.
        The behavior of an app is determined by its program code.</p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.6</title>
      <p>An app should behave as intended across a range of conditions,
        including different ways people may interact with it.</p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.7</title>
      <p>Users can understand an app’s purpose and behavior by using it and observing how it
        responds to their actions.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Inputs and Outputs</title>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.1</title>
      <p>Apps are designed to take in information from a variety of sources, process that
        information, and respond in useful ways.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.2</title>
      <p>When users interact with apps, they often tap or click buttons, type letters or numbers,
        speak, or move the device.
        Each of these actions provides input: data sent to a computer for a program to process.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.3</title>
      <p>Apps should provide clear feedback when they receive input, such as highlighting a button,
        showing a loading icon, playing a sound, vibrating, or displaying an error message.
        This feedback is a form of output that helps users know the app received the input, is
        working, and what step to take next.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.4</title>
      <p>Input often comes from a user’s direct actions, but it can also be captured automatically
        from sources such as sensors (like an accelerometer), location, time, or network signals.
        Apps may also receive audio or images from microphones and cameras, often through user
        actions (like a voice command or face scan).
        These inputs allow apps to provide features like maps, step counting, face filters, or
        recommendations.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.5</title>
      <p>In many apps, the program responds to events, such as button clicks, key presses, or sensor
        updates, by running specific code.
        Events are one way input is delivered to a program. An event signals that something
        happened, and an event handler processes the input and determines what happens next.</p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.6</title>
      <p>Outputs are what an app produces. Outputs may include text, images, sounds, or something
        the user feels, like a vibration.
        Some outputs are directly perceived by the user, while others are not, such as data written
        to a file or sent over a network.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.7</title>
      <p>Users can identify inputs and outputs by trying different actions in an app and observing
        what the app displays or does in response.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
</section>